    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `run_tool` in `opsbot/calls.py`. The 3 existing tests pass, and a separate check with a fake session passed for all three cases.\n\n```python\ndef _text(result: CallToolResult) -> str:\n    return \"\\n\".join(block.text for block in result.content if isinstance(block, TextContent))\n\n\nasync def run_tool(session: ClientSession, name: str, arguments: dict[str, Any]) -> Any:\n    result = await session.call_tool(name, arguments)\n    if result.is_error:\n        raise ToolCallFailed(name, _text(result))\n    if result.structured_content is not None:\n        return result.structured_content\n    return _text(result)\n```\n\n- **Failed call:** if the server marks the result as an error, it raises `ToolCallFailed(name, message)`. The message is the result's text blocks joined with `\"\\n\"`, and any structured output is ignored.\n- **Structured output:** returned as-is whenever it is present, even if it's an empty value like `[]` or `{}`. The one exception is a JSON `null`: the library stores \"no structured output\" as `None`, so a tool that returns `null` falls through to the text result.\n- **Text:** only text blocks are joined; images and other blocks are skipped.\n- **Protocol errors:** if the server rejects the request outright (for example, an unknown tool name), the library raises `MCPError`, not `ToolCallFailed`. The CLI doesn't catch that, so it will show a traceback instead of the tidy `error:` line.\n\nI didn't add any dependencies.\n\nThe throwaway check script `_check_run_tool.py` is still in the project root because deleting it needed your approval. You can delete it."
